sec.p:
As there is a ‘1’ at positions {4}, {6}, {14}, and {15}, state {1, 13} can make four different binding transitions to new states containing these basis states, and in reverse, dissociations take place to transition to state {1, 13}:


{1,13}⇌{1,4,13}{1,13}⇌{1,6,13}{1,13}⇌{1,13,14}{1,13}⇌{1,13,15}


(9)

where in this isomerization the divalently bound ligand is dissociating from node d or e. Testing {13} results in no other isomerizations. Hence the final result is that {3, 12, 13} isomerizes to {3, 13, 14} and {3, 13, 15}, where because basis states are ordered numerically, {14} and {15} are written after {13} in the new states, though they are isomerization of {12}:


{3,12,13}⇌{3,13,14}{3,12,13}⇌{3,13,15}


(10)
